  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nIn the wake of a large-scale disturbance, heavy rainfall (2 to 4\ninches) has fallen south of Pittsburgh. High-water impacts may\ncontinue for a number of communities despite the loss of rain\nintensity and coverage early this morning. \n\nGiven that the Allegheny River and Youghiogheny River Basin \nwere spare intensity rainfall amounts, the likelihood that \nPittsburgh will be impacted (flooding at the Mon Wharf Parking\nLot) is considered low. \n\nDry and quiet weather is expected Wednesday afternoon through\nFriday under high pressure. The probability of hazardous weather\nis considered very low.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe threat for severe thunderstorms could return early next week\nas an upper-level ridge shifts from the West Coast towards the \nGreat Lakes and southwesterly low-level flow transports warm, \nmoist air into the region. \n\nLong-range guidance suggests one or more mesoscale convective\nsystems (MCSs) may develop over the Midwest before tracking into\nthe Ohio River Valley. Forecast soundings indicate an\nenvironment capable of supporting all severe weather hazards,\nincluding large hail, damaging wind gusts, tornadoes, and heavy\nrainfall. Machine learning guidance currently indicates a 5-30%\nprobability of severe thunderstorms from Sunday through Tuesday\nacross the Ohio River Valley. The eventual evolution of this\nthreat will largely depend on the amplitude of the eastern U.S.\ntrough and the strength and position of the upper-level ridge,\nwhich will influence the track and intensity of any MCSs.",
